Finish the session-first workspace migration and remove obsolete task flow

The desktop app had drifted into a split state: the main UX was already session/workspace-first, but legacy task/attempt routes, dialogs, and task-only panels were still wired into navigation, persisted window routes, and parts of the workspace shell. In parallel, workspace binding and desktop side windows needed hardening so the new model behaved consistently across terminals, repo views, notifications, and project rail interactions.

This commit removes the obsolete task-form/task-attempt frontend chain, migrates project entry points and persisted routes to session/workspace paths, updates workspace-aware panels to read the current workspace directly, and keeps the remaining shared components that are still used by the session flow. It also includes the backend and regression fixes needed to keep worktree creation, terminal attachment, desktop toast windows, and repository/workspace path binding aligned with the session-first model.

Constraint: User-facing workspace/session behavior must prioritize the current model over backward-compatible task UI shims
Constraint: Existing session features still reuse some task-named shared components, so cleanup had to remove only dead task-specific surfaces
Rejected: Keep legacy /tasks route compatibility alongside new routes | preserves split state and stale entry points
Rejected: Add redirect wrappers around old task dialogs and panels | masks the wrong model instead of deleting it
